1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,150
Kant's emphasis on human dignity has led him

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:06,300
to be called the father of human rights,

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,560
and his influence remains strong in modern Germany.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:14,350
The first article of the Constitution declares that

5
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:18,300
human dignity shall be inviolable, never to be compromised.

6
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:24,170
But what happens when respecting someone's dignity prevents

7
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:27,660
us from acting to save an innocent life?

8
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:34,860
In 2002, Jakob von Metzler, the 11-year-old son

9
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:42,060
of a prominent German banking family, was kidnapped.

10
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:46,730
A few days later, the police arrested Magnus

11
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:49,940
Gaffgen, after he had collected the ransom money.

12
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:53,840
But he refused to say where his victim was hidden.

13
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:57,640
The deputy police chief of Frankfurt.

14
00:00:57,660 --> 00:01:01,080
He told this kidnapper that if he

15
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:04,500
doesn't tell where the child is hidden,

16
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:07,080
he would suffer in a

17
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:09,500
way that he cannot even imagine.

18
00:01:11,259 --> 00:01:13,420
He threatened him with torture.

19
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:16,800
The threat worked.

20
00:01:18,259 --> 00:01:20,329
Gaffgen admitted that he had already

21
00:01:20,329 --> 00:01:22,400
killed the boy and hidden the body.

22
00:01:25,039 --> 00:01:27,400
He was given a life sentence for murder.

23
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:32,660
But remarkably, the deputy police chief was also

24
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:37,660
prosecuted and convicted of violating the kidnapper's rights.

25
00:01:41,259 --> 00:01:43,259
You're trying to save an innocent child, and

26
00:01:43,259 --> 00:01:45,259
here you have the criminal who kidnapped him.

27
00:01:45,259 --> 00:01:48,759
The argument against it is that there

28
00:01:48,759 --> 00:01:52,259
are some inherent qualities in a person

29
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:54,759
that the person cannot forfeit even

30
00:01:54,759 --> 00:01:57,259
by doing the worst deeds possible.

31
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:03,160
According to Kantian ethics, you're not allowed to just use a

32
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:08,660
person, to just abuse him, to hurt him, to torture him,

33
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:11,160
in order to get something out of him,

34
00:02:11,160 --> 00:02:13,660
even if the purpose of this was good.

35
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,660
Because that's using a person as a

36
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:19,660
means, rather than respecting him as an end.

37
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:20,660
Exactly.

38
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:22,660
Even though he's a criminal, a kidnapper.

39
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:23,960
Even though it's a criminal, even though we

40
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:25,260
think he didn't really act terribly, you know, nice,

41
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:26,260
even though he's a criminal, even though we

42
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:27,260
think he didn't really act terribly, you know, nice,

43
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:28,160
even though he's a criminal, even

44
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:28,660
though he's a criminal, you know, nice,

45
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:30,160
and he didn't really have

46
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,660
much dignity in his own actions,

47
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:33,160
why should you treat him,

48
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:34,660
you know, with respect and dignity?

49
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:35,660
Exactly.

50
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:37,660
You are not allowed to treat a

51
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:39,660
person as a means for another end.

52
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:41,660
Now, here's what a utilitarian would say.

53
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:44,660
A utilitarian would say,

54
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:50,660
you've defended Kant, on his categorical principle,

55
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:53,660
but you've just shown what's

56
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:56,660
morally absurd about the Kantian position.

57
00:02:56,660 --> 00:02:57,160
But you've just shown what's

58
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:57,660
morally absurd about the Kantian position.

59
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:02,300
Within the utilitarian way of thinking about

60
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:06,940
moral issues or moral cases, you cannot distinguish

61
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:10,009
in the end anymore what kind of action

62
00:03:10,009 --> 00:03:13,079
is good and what kind of action is bad.

63
00:03:13,079 --> 00:03:16,200
It's totally relative.

64
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,730
In some instances it's good to torture,

65
00:03:18,730 --> 00:03:21,260
in other instances it's not good to torture.

66
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:23,220
What about respect for human dignity?

67
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:25,060
Well, again, I would say what about

68
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:26,900
respect for the dignity of the child?

69
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:30,900
I mean, here's a child who is locked up

70
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:34,900
somewhere, going to die slowly from hunger and thirst.

71
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:36,510
There's no way that's a dignified

72
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:38,120
thing to do to the child.

73
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:41,080
As a utilitarian, I would say, if I know that

74
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:44,040
I can save the child and I don't, then I'm

75
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:46,760
responsible for that child's death.

76
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:49,500
And that's what, in my view, Kantians

77
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:52,240
refuse to acknowledge, their responsibility for the

78
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:54,960
things that they don't do that could save lives.

79
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:56,840
Now, in the German case...

80
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:59,500
The kidnapping case, they were confident

81
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:02,160
that they had identified the perpetrator.

82
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:05,410
Let's assume that's the case, but the

83
00:04:05,410 --> 00:04:08,660
perpetrator still won't talk, even under torture.

84
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:15,300
But he would talk if you tortured his 14-year-old daughter.

85
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,300
Would you do it?

86
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:18,519
I mean, that would be a much harder

87
00:04:18,519 --> 00:04:20,740
case, you know, in an emotional level, I think,

88
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:23,010
to torture someone who you know has done

89
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:25,280
something horrible is something that you can psychologically

90
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:26,840
come at more easily than to torture.

91
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:30,119
I mean, you can torture somebody who's completely innocent.

92
00:04:30,119 --> 00:04:33,239
So if it's simply the one-on-one case here,

93
00:04:33,239 --> 00:04:36,359
I would say no, because the child that you're

94
00:04:36,359 --> 00:04:38,919
torturing is just as innocent as the child who's dying.

95
00:04:38,919 --> 00:04:40,340
But if there are 10 children who are...

96
00:04:40,340 --> 00:04:44,129
But if you up the numbers, I suppose I'm

97
00:04:44,129 --> 00:04:47,919
going to come under a lot of pressure, and perhaps

98
00:04:47,919 --> 00:04:50,229
I will say, I don't know if I could do

99
00:04:50,229 --> 00:04:52,539
it, but perhaps I would say, if you really knew

100
00:04:52,539 --> 00:04:54,349
that that was going to get

101
00:04:54,349 --> 00:04:56,159
the information to save the 10 children.

102
00:04:56,159 --> 00:04:56,719
Yeah.

103
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:59,200
Then you would...

104
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:00,880
Then the right thing to do would

105
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:02,560
be to torture one to save 10.

106
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:04,780
Even an innocent girl?

107
00:05:04,780 --> 00:05:07,140
She's innocent, but so are the 10 innocent, of course.

108
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:08,140
And it's a matter of numbers.

109
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:10,419
And it's a matter of numbers in the end.

110
00:05:10,419 --> 00:05:15,249
As a war reporter, I have

111
00:05:15,249 --> 00:05:20,080
to say, you know, I can see...

112
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:22,399
I speak to people who were

113
00:05:22,399 --> 00:05:24,719
victims of this kind of thinking.

114
00:05:24,719 --> 00:05:25,840
You know, if you...

115
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:26,840
If you...

116
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:31,930
If you talk to people who were tortured,

117
00:05:31,930 --> 00:05:37,020
badly tortured, exactly with that kind of argument,

118
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:41,920
it's so evident why you need Kantian thinking

119
00:05:41,920 --> 00:05:46,820
as the guidance, per se, to stop people from

120
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:49,419
thinking they could use others as a means.

121
00:05:49,419 --> 00:05:51,080
It's for me, that's...

122
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:53,239
You know, it's...

123
00:05:53,239 --> 00:05:54,739
I see on every single

124
00:05:54,739 --> 00:05:56,239
trip I make to whichever country.

125
00:05:56,240 --> 00:06:00,800
Wherever I speak to people who, you know,

126
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:05,360
were abused, who were tortured, who were mistreated

127
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:08,060
with such kind of argument that it's...

128
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:09,060
It's full purpose.

129
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:12,360
There's a good end to this.

130
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:15,180
There's a reason why we could torture people.

131
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:17,020
It's devastating to see that.

132
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:18,020
So I'm...

133
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:19,020
You know, I'm deep...

134
00:06:19,020 --> 00:06:22,320
I'm deeply convinced that Kantian thinking is...

135
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:24,240
Is the best guidance we have to protect human rights.

136
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:25,240
And I think that's...

137
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:26,240
I think that's...

138
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:27,240
I think that's the key to our rights.

139
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:31,240
The von Metzler case prompted much

140
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:35,240
debate over Germany's constitutional commitment to

141
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:36,240
human dignity.

